1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 Decision trees. When do we use decision trees? Well one thing which is certain, we use decision trees if we have a risk situation.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 And we will work with probability estimations for the outcomes and the measure of words, our present word that we had before will become an expected value.

3
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 So the expected present word. And another thing which is typical for decision trees is that we work with more than one stage.

4
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 So we have to make a decision and then at the next stage we will have to make another decision.

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 This is a part of a decision tree. There are two different nodes possible in decision trees. By the way I think most of you have encountered decision trees before.

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 But let me explain again. So there is a decision node. So you, decision maker, take a decision or alternative weights you can go.

7
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 And then something happens. Nature, again nature takes a decision. But it's not like nature takes a decision. I don't know what.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
 So you know what the probabilities are, you know that there are different outcomes and you know what the probabilities for these different outcomes are.

9
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 And then you take it as well that you have to take. And we talk about stage decisions. Here we have to take another decision.

10
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 And we have alternatives and again nature will decide and we can have different outcomes. Here again same thing here, similar.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 So that's a decision tree. And what we will do, we will try to solve the decision tree in order to decide what is the best course of action.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 Let's have a look at this example. So with this case we are with Jerry and Jerry is the CEO of a food processing company.

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 And recently he was approached by a supermarket chain that wants to market its frozen dinners.

14
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 And their offer forces Jerry to make two decisions. One decision now and one decision within two years.

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 The current decision that he has to take is either to lease a factory or to own one.

16
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 Now there are possibilities of course, there are probabilities. We don't know if it will be success.

17
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 So here, well market can be favorable or not. Same here. If, well we are in the lease option, we have to make a decision.

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 And then the decision is if we have a good outcome we will have to decide whether or not to expand our facility.

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 Might times two, times one or maybe half it. And here if it's not favorable we can downsize our activities or we can just stop and go out of business.

20
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 If we decide to own the facility, again it can be good or it can be bad, we will take another decision here.

21
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 Whether we want to quadruple our activity or just double it, stay the same or stop.

22
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Again we can have a good or bad outcome but we know these probabilities. That's an important thing.

23
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 Okay, but maybe, I hope it's clear but let's have a look at another example maybe. That will make things even more clearer.

24
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 Here, this example I will explain step by step how decision tree is built and how it's solved.

25
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 I added a few slides with the steps in order to make things more clear.

26
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
 What is it about? Well we need to make a decision about either marketing or selling a new invention. So you see, market, sell.

27
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
 If the product is marketed, a next decision is to take it international or stay national.

28
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 And the outcome of the branch is result in this decision tree. Probabilities are given and the question is determine the best choice.

29
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 Now let's take it step by step. Important in decision trees is that we build it from the left to the right and we will solve it from the right to the left.

30
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 Step by step, so we have to decide either we are building the tree now, we have to decide whether we market or sell.

31
00:04:51,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 Then out of our hands but if we sell well it's over for us but if we decide to market it we can have either a favorable or an unfavorable outcome.

32
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 We will have to make a next decision which can be either to go international or to stay national.

33
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:27,000
 We know that there is a 20% probability here that market outcome will be favorable and 80% that it will be low.

34
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:35,000
 We take another decision, go international or go national and we have the probabilities what will happen.

35
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 If we go international and we are in this situation, these are the probabilities.

36
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:54,000
 We have a probability of 50% of having this present word or cash flow before taxes and 50% chance to have this outcome.

37
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 And here we will have these probabilities each time with the outcome and here again we have probabilities and outcome.

38
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 Of course, and also here we have a possible outcome but here we are sure that we will have this 9.

39
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 Now we are done with building the tree, now we have to start solving it.

40
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 And how do we solve it? Well the first step that we take is this one, so we start here.

41
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 So in this particular point if we go that direction we will have 14 and 14 is 0.5 times 12 plus 0.5 times 16 is 6 plus 8 is 14.

42
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:53,000
 In a similar way we compute this 0.2 and here similar way we compute this.

43
00:06:53,000 --> 00:07:06,000
 And we see that if we have to take a decision here then apparently the most favorable one is to go international and here as well we go international.

44
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 Brings us to the next step.

45
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 Ok, well here is the computations. So we will go for 14 and we will go for 4.2.

46
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
 We have to move on.

47
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 Now we have to decide, well we have this situation so we have to decide there.

48
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 And here the 6.16, how do we come to 6.16? Well it's 0.2 times 14 plus 0.8 times 4.2, 6.16.

49
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:50,000
 So if we go this way we have an expected present word of 6.16.

50
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,000
 If we go that way we have an expected outcome of 9.

51
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 So it would be wise here to take the decision and to sell.

52
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:12,000
 So that's how it works. We will see some examples in the workshops. I will have a larger exercise at the end of this chapter.

53
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:16,000
 So that's how decision trees work. So it's not that complicated.

54
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
 You have to carefully build your decision tree and then carefully make the computations.

55
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 Ok, now decision trees are not only interesting to visualize these stage decisions.

56
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 We can also use them for informational analysis.

57
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:45,000
 We can use them to obtain some more insights in the situation.

58
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
 And there are three things that we can compute.

59
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
 We can compute the value of control, we can compute the value of perfect information and the value of imperfect information.

60
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:03,000
 Basically the value means, well what would you be willing to suppose that somebody can offer you a perfect forecast.

61
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,000
 What would you be willing to pay for that?

62
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:14,000
 Or suppose that that forecast is not 100% accurate but only 80% accurate.

63
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
 Then what would you be willing to pay for that?

64
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
 And the value of control, suppose that you have control over nature, then what would you do?

65
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:29,000
 Ok, sounds maybe a little bit strange but it's something that is often used.

66
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
 So here you have the definitions.

67
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:41,000
 As I said, value of perfect information, expected value if you had perfect information, minus expected value without perfect information.

68
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:47,000
 Ok, and this is similar for imperfect and value of control but let's have a look at an example.

69
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:58,000
 Let's have a look at an example. It will not only show how we compute value of perfect information, value of imperfect information and value of control.

70
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:04,000
 But it also introduces a concept which is called influence diagram.

71
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:13,000
 And the influence diagram is often used to visualize the decision situation for making decisions.

72
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:22,000
 What are the components? The components are, well we have a value model which is here, the net benefit.

73
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:32,000
 And the net benefit here, it's the crop value minus frost protection cost, let me first explain.

74
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:44,000
 It's about an orange grower and the orange grower can either decide to buy and install protection against frost or not.

75
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 Frost can or cannot occur overnight and if there is frost, obviously that will have an impact on the quality of the oranges.

76
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:05,000
 Ok, now we have, this is our value model, the net benefit and this is the decision we have to take.

77
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:11,000
 Whether or not we will buy and install that frost protection, obviously that is costing money.

78
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:23,000
 So that frost protection will have an influence on the crop value, it will have an influence on of course the frost protection cost, buy or not.

79
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:32,000
 And the crop value is not only determined here by our frost protection, we are sure if we buy it what will be the value when we have frost.

80
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
 But what we don't know is whether we will have frost or not.

81
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:43,000
 So this is what an influence diagram is like, so it helps to analyze and structure the decision problem.

82
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
 We will see another example in the case study.

83
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
 Ok, now let's build the tree.

84
00:11:51,000 --> 00:12:01,000
 So if we know that frost protection cost is 25, euros, dollars, whatever, value of undamaged cost is 100,

85
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:11,000
 value of crop if we have frost but we have frost protection will be 75, if there is frost and we have no frost protection everything will be lost.

86
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:22,000
 And we know from historical data on the weather that the probability of having frost is 0.4 and the probability of having no frost is 0.6.

87
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 So we start building our tree like we did before, the decision is to have frost protection or not.

88
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
 And then nature decides whether we will have frost or not.

89
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
 Nature decides.

90
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:41,000
 We know these probabilities, 0.4, 0.6, 0.4, 0.6.

91
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
 Now we have to compute these values.

92
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
 So we say what is the value?

93
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
 Well here we are in a situation where we have frost protection.

94
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:54,000
 Ok, that means that we have bought at frost protection.

95
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:01,000
 Frost protection which is costing 25.

96
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:07,000
 But we also know that our crop will be protected so we will have this 75.

97
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
 So 75 minus 25 is 50.

98
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
 And in a similar way we compute these outcomes.

99
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 So now we have built the tree, now we have to solve it.

100
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
 So we start from the right to the left here.

101
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,000
 If we go that way we have 0.4 times 50.

102
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,000
 That way we have 0.6 times 75 is 65.

103
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
 So we have 65 here.

104
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
 Here, well 0.4 times 0 plus 0.6 times 100 is 60.

105
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
 This is higher, so the expected value is higher.

106
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:47,000
 So recommendation is invest in frost protection.

107
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:52,000
 Now here we see the value of control.

108
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:03,000
 The value of control is well we are capable of influencing the weather.

109
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:07,000
 So we will choose to have no frost.

110
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,000
 And then still we can buy to have frost protection.

111
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:16,000
 Frost protection but there is no frost.

112
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
 That means that still we will have 75.

113
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:25,000
 No frost protection and no frost we will have 100 because we will not have spent any money.

114
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 So it seems that if we could control the weather then it would be, well we could go.

115
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
 We would go that way.

116
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
 If there is no frost we would not install frost protection.

117
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 This is what our previous tree was telling us.

118
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
 This is what this is telling us.

119
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:51,000
 So that means that if we would be able to control the weather we would be willing to pay 35 euros for it.

120
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
 Now the value of perfect information.

121
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
 The value of perfect information.

122
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
 Look we have a change in our influence diagram.

123
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
 We will have a different tree structure.

124
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
 So what do we have?

125
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:10,000
 We have perfect information.

126
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 So we still have, we know what will happen.

127
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:21,000
 Of course these probabilities remain 0.4 and 0.6 as we cannot influence nature here.

128
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:30,000
 But if we have frost, investing in frost protection would result in the net benefit of 50.

129
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:35,000
 If there is frost and we have no frost protection we would have zero benefit.

130
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
 And in case of no frost we have 75 and 100.

131
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:44,000
 So here in this case we would have 100.

132
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,000
 If we know that we have perfect information.

133
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
 And here if we knew that there was no frost we would do this.

134
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
 If we knew that there was frost we would go for this option.

135
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
 So we would have here an expected value of 50.

136
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:05,000
 And then here, well similar way, we have 0.4 times 50 plus 0.6 times 100 is 80.

137
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
 So our previous tree, what we have now.

138
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:14,000
 So we would be willing to pay 15 for perfect information.

139
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:23,000
 So it's interesting, suppose that somebody is offering you that information and is saying well I charge you 10 for it.

140
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
 You should definitely do so.

141
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 If somebody is offering that information and is saying I'm charging you 30 for it.

142
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:36,000
 You should not do it because that information is not that valuable.

143
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:44,000
 Now of course if we have perfect information we can also have a situation of imperfect information.

144
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:49,000
 Here we introduce a new variable in our decision tree.

145
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:55,000
 And the new variable in our decision tree is the forecast.

146
00:16:55,000 --> 00:17:00,000
 Before we can solve our decision tree we have to transform it.

147
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
 So this is nature's probability tree.

148
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:09,000
 So we have 40% and 60%.

149
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
 Now if we have here the quotes it means the forecast.

150
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
 And we have a forecast that is 80% correct.

151
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:25,000
 So that means that if there is actually frost our forecast will have set frost with 80% probability.

152
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
 And 20% probability that it will have set no frost.

153
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:41,000
 If there is no frost our forecasting model will have told us frost 20% or will have correctly mentioned no frost with 80%.

154
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:49,000
 So 0.4 times 0.8 is 0.32, 0.4 times 0.2 is 0.08.

155
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:58,000
 Now what we have to do is of course since we are talking about having information we will have to transform that tree.

156
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
 So we will have to transform it in the decision maker's probability tree.

157
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:10,000
 And here we start in the tree with the forecast that is saying us frost or no frost.

158
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:17,000
 And we have 0.44 because that's the sum of the here frost frost.

159
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
 So the 0.12 and the 0.32.

160
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
 And here the no frost is then the sum of this one and this one.

161
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:29,000
 And we have to deal with posterior probabilities.

162
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:35,000
 And I think you all know that is using the rules of Bayes.

163
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:45,000
 And okay once we have these probabilities we can put them in our tree.

164
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:51,000
 And we can work in a similar way as we did before.

165
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:56,000
 And go from the right to the left.

166
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:05,000
 And here we have the value of imperfect information is 8.13 which is of course lower than the value of perfect information.

167
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:19,000
 Important to note here is that you have to adjust your initial tree because the situation is different for value of control, value of perfect, value of imperfect information.

168
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:31,000
 And interesting to notice is also here that we can visualize the change decision situation by adjusting our influence diagram.

169
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
 So that influence diagram is quite powerful.

170
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:48,000
 Maybe you find this situation still rather simple but if the situation gets a little bit more complex it's quite interesting to have an influence diagram.

171
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,000
 We will see another example in the case study.

